Homolytic bond fission takes place in free radical chlorination of methane.<br>\[
\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\mathrm{H}+\mathrm{Cl}_{2} \stackrel{520-670 \mathrm{~K}}{\text { or hv }} \gt \mathrm{CH}_{3}-\mathrm{Cl}+\mathrm{HCl}
\]<br>The reaction occurs by a free radical mechanism which involves the following \( 3 \) steps:<br>Chain initiation<br><img src="/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkcet%252Fsol_316.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2" data-qx-orig-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkcet%2Fsol_316.png?alt=media" data-qx-storage-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkcet%2Fsol_316.png?alt=media"><br>Chain propagation<br>\[
\begin{array}{l}
\mathrm{CH}_{4}+\underset{\text { Methane }}{\mathrm{Cl}}+\underset{\text { Chlorine free radical }}{\mathrm{Cl}} \mathbf{~} \mathbf{~ H}_{3}+\mathrm{H} \mathrm{Cl} \\
\mathrm{CH}_{3}+\mathrm{Cl}-\mathrm{Cl} \stackrel{\mathrm{hv}}{\rightarrow} \mathrm{CH}_{3}-\mathrm{Cl}+\mathrm{Cl} \\
\mathrm{CH}_{3} \mathrm{Cl}+\mathrm{Cl} \rightarrow \mathrm{CH}_{2} \mathrm{Cl}+\mathrm{HCl} \\
\mathrm{CH}_{2} \mathrm{Cl}+\mathrm{Cl}-\mathrm{Cl} \rightarrow \mathrm{CH}_{2} \mathrm{Cl}_{2}+\mathrm{Cl}
\end{array}
\]<br>Chain termination<br>\[
\begin{array}{l}
\mathrm{Cl}+\mathrm{Cl} \rightarrow \mathrm{Cl}-\mathrm{Cl} \\
\mathrm{CH}_{3}+\mathrm{CH}_{3} \rightarrow \mathrm{CH}_{3}-\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\
\text { (Ethane) } \\
\mathrm{CH}_{3}+\mathrm{Cl} \rightarrow \mathrm{CH}_{3}-\mathrm{Cl}
\end{array}
\]

Percentage of nitrogen

\begin{aligned}
&=\frac{1.4 \times \mathrm{N} \text { of acid } \times \mathrm{Vol} \text {. used with } \mathrm{NH}_{3}}{\text { Weight of organic compound }} \\
&=\frac{1.4 \times 1 \times 30}{1.2} \\
&=35
\end{aligned}

From Kjeldahl's method, percentage of nitrogen

\begin{aligned}
&=\frac{1.4 \times \mathrm{N} \times \mathrm{V}}{\mathrm{w}}=\frac{1.4 \times 0.1 \times 30}{5} \\
&=0.84 \%
\end{aligned}

When carbon is bonded to four other atoms, the angle between any pair of bonds $=109^{\circ}, 28^{\prime}$ (tetrahedral angle) but the ring of cyclobutane is square with four angles of $90^{\circ}$.
So, deviation of the bond angle (angle strain) in cyclobutane $=109^{\circ} 28^{\prime}-90^{\circ} / 2$
=19^{\circ} 28^{\prime} / 2=9^{\circ} 44^{\prime}

$\mathrm{CH}_{3} \mathrm{OCH}_{3}, \quad \mathrm{C}_{2} \mathrm{H}_{5} \mathrm{OH}\begin{array}{cc}\begin{array}{c}\text { Methoxy methane } \\ \text { (alcohol) }\end{array} & \text { Ethanol (ether) } \\ \text { functional group }\end{array}
$\begin{array}{ll}\left(\mathrm{C}_{2} \mathrm{H}_{6} \mathrm{O}\right) & \left(\mathrm{C}_{2} \mathrm{H}_{6} \mathrm{O}\right) \text { molecular formula }\end{array}
In methoxy methane and ethanol both molecular formula is same but functional groups are different, so they are functional isomers.

Angle strain in cyclopropane is $24^{\circ} 44^{\prime}$
\begin{aligned}
\theta &=\left[180-\frac{360}{n}\right] \\
&=\left[180-\frac{360}{3}\right]=180-120=60^{\circ}
\end{aligned}

\text { Angle strain } \begin{aligned}
\alpha &=\frac{1}{2}\left[109^{\circ} 28^{\circ}-\theta\right] \\
&=\frac{1}{2}\left[109^{\circ} 28^{\prime}-60^{\circ}\right] \\
&=24^{\circ} 44^{\prime}
\end{aligned}
